@page "/graph-gallery"

<CatHead Title="Graph gallery">
    Whole graphs for real jobs. Each is one <code>[data-graph]</code> with the frame around it that the
    job needs &mdash; a toolbar, filters, a legend, a tooltip, a side panel &mdash; built from the markup on
    <a href="/graph">Graph</a>, <a href="/graph-layouts">Graph layouts</a>,
    <a href="/graph-exploring">Exploring a graph</a> and <a href="/graph-plugins">Graph plugins</a>.
    Nothing here is a component and nothing needs a script of your own: copy one, replace the list with
    your records, and it draws.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <i class="ri-share-circle-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">The list is the data, and the text alternative</span>
        Every example's <code>.graph-data</code> is what the canvas draws and what a screen reader reads,
        so each record and each link is written as a sentence. Your app renders the same list from its
        own records. Past a few hundred, hand them over with <code>data-graph-src</code> or
        <a href="/graph-blazor"><code>SetDataAsync</code></a> instead of rendering markup.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.OrgChart" Title="An org chart" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>dagre</code> from the top, <code>data-graph-nodes="box"</code> so every name is read, and
        <code>data-graph-curve="taxi"</code> for the right angles an org chart is expected to have. The
        role goes in <code>data-meta</code>, which a box writes under the name. Links carry no label,
        so <code>data-graph-edge-labels="none"</code> keeps the sentences off the canvas.
    </p>
    <p>
        A dotted-line report is one more link with <code>data-line="dotted"</code>. Draw it between
        adjacent levels: a hierarchy layout weighs every link the same, so a link that skips a level pulls
        the person it starts from down a rank. The search marks people rather than hiding the rest, and
        both downloads take the whole chart, not just the part in view.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.ServiceMap" Title="A service map with health" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        One <code>data-group</code> per team, laid out as <code>islands</code> and outlined with
        <code>data-graph-hulls</code>. The record whose id is the group's name is the team itself: it
        anchors its island and gives the outline its colour. Health is the tone &mdash;
        <code>go</code>, <code>warn</code>, <code>danger</code> &mdash; and the team's colour is a second
        one, <code>data-tone-team</code>, which the <strong>Health / Team</strong> switch chooses between.
        The words are in <code>data-meta</code> as well, so the tooltip, the search and the announcements
        say the status too.
    </p>
    <p>
        The team chips are the legend for the islands and the filter at once. <code>data-hub</code> on
        the services that are failing writes their names in bold and puts them first when names compete
        for room, and <code>data-graph-spacing="compact"</code> keeps each island tight enough for every
        name to fit beside it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.NetworkTopology" Title="A network by site and rack" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Sites and racks are records too, and <code>data-parent</code> draws a host inside its rack and a
        rack inside its site. <code>fcose</code> is the layout that understands nested groups, and
        <code>data-graph-collapse</code> lets a reader fold a rack or a whole site away &mdash; with the
        cue on the group, or all at once from the toolbar.
    </p>
    <p>
        Addresses are in <code>data-meta</code>, so typing <kbd class="kbd">198.51</kbd> into the search
        marks the south site. The tooltip is the app's own <code>.graph-tip</code>, filled from
        <code>data-role</code> and <code>data-meta</code>; any <code>data-*</code> on a record is a field
        a slot can name.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.KnowledgeGraph" Title="A knowledge graph" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Notes, tasks, people and tags, each kind with its own shape as well as its own colour, filed into
        one island per project. People and tags belong to no project, so they sit between the islands
        they join. The kind chips are the legend and the switch, and <strong>Done tasks</strong> shows or
        hides the records marked <code>data-muted</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Select a record and the side panel lists what it touches, one row per neighbour cloned from the
        app's <code>&lt;template data-graph-neighbour&gt;</code>; a row selects that neighbour, so the
        reader can walk the graph from the keyboard alone. Right-click a record, or press
        <kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">F10</kbd> on it, for the menu. Names give way to
        each other at this size &mdash; zoom in, point at a record or search to read the rest.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.StateMachine" Title="A state machine" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>dagre</code> left to right, boxes, arrows, and the event on every transition with
        <code>data-graph-edge-labels="always"</code>. The start and the two ends are
        <code>data-display="dot"</code> in a graph of boxes; the failure is <code>danger</code>, the
        exceptions are dashed, and <code>data-root</code> rings the state this order is in.
        <code>data-graph-drag="false"</code> keeps a diagram people learn by its shape from being pulled
        apart.
    </p>
    <p>
        Keep event names short. A transition's label sits between two states on a link no longer than the
        gap between them, and a long one covers its own arrow.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.DependencyTree" Title="A dependency tree" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Versions in <code>data-meta</code>, deprecated packages <code>data-muted</code> behind a
        <code>data-graph-show="muted"</code> checkbox, and build-only packages behind a chip on their own
        field, <code>data-scope</code>. The two radios are <code>data-graph-option="direction"</code>, so
        the same tree reads down or across without a script.
    </p>
    <p>
        A package two others depend on is one record with two links in, which is what makes it a graph and
        not a tree &mdash; <code>log-core</code> here. The advisory is a <code>danger</code> tone and an
        icon and words in the box, never the colour alone.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.DataLineage" Title="Data lineage" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Sources, staging, models, marts and reports, left to right with
        <code>data-graph-curve="round-taxi"</code>. Each record carries two colourings: its layer as
        <code>data-tone</code> and its freshness as <code>data-tone-freshness</code>. Switch to
        <strong>Freshness</strong> and the failed load shows how far it reached &mdash; the two legends
        are both on screen, because a legend is markup and the switch does not rewrite it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.MindMap" Title="A mind map" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>rings</code> around the <code>data-root</code> topic, the branches weighted larger with
        <code>data-weight</code> and named in bold with <code>data-hub</code>, each branch in a tone its
        links repeat. <code>data-graph-spacing="compact"</code> keeps each branch's ideas close to it and
        leaves room between the branches for every name. Keep ideas short: a name that would run into
        another is left out until the reader zooms in.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.BlastRadius" Title="The blast radius of an incident" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        The failing store is the root, and <code>data-graph-focus</code> with
        <code>data-graph-focus-depth="2"</code> starts on everything within two hops of it. The hop radios
        are <code>data-graph-option="depth"</code>; <strong>Whole map</strong> is
        <code>data-graph-action="unfocus"</code>, and <strong>Back to the failure</strong> is a
        <code>focus</code> action with the record's id as its <code>value</code>. <code>dagre</code> puts
        the callers above what they call, so the failure is at the bottom and the damage climbs.
    </p>
    <p>
        The stats line counts what is in view against the whole, in the app's words. It sits in the card's
        body rather than in the graph, and <code>data-graph-frame</code> on the card is what ties it and
        the controls to this graph.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphGallery.LargeMap" Title="A large map" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Every service at once, as <code>islands</code>: one per domain, each shaped by its own springs with
        the domain's record at the centre, and the domains of one <code>data-cluster</code> packed side by
        side. At this size the
        shape is the point, so names are placed the way a map places them &mdash; the domains first, the
        rest as zooming makes room. The minimap keeps the reader's place, and switching every platform off
        shows the app's own <code>.graph-empty--filtered</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The records here are markup so the example stays one file. A real map of this size comes from
        <code>data-graph-src</code>, which the browser fetches itself, or from
        <a href="/graph-blazor"><code>SetDataAsync</code></a>; a list rendered by Blazor is held in the
        circuit and diffed on every change.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="choosing">Which one to start from</h2>

<table class="table">
    <thead>
        <tr><th>The job</th><th>Layout</th><th>Records</th><th>Around it</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Who reports to whom</td><td><code>dagre</code>, <code>taxi</code></td><td>Boxes</td><td>Search, legend, export</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What calls what, and what is broken</td><td><code>islands</code>, hulls</td><td>Dots</td><td>Team chips, colour switch, tooltip</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What is inside what</td><td><code>fcose</code>, <code>data-parent</code></td><td>Dots</td><td>Fold buttons, search by address</td></tr>
        <tr><td>How things relate, to explore</td><td><code>islands</code></td><td>Dots</td><td>Kind chips, search, side panel, menu</td></tr>
        <tr><td>How something moves between states</td><td><code>dagre</code> across</td><td>Boxes, labelled links</td><td>Legend</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What depends on what</td><td><code>dagre</code></td><td>Boxes</td><td>Direction, deprecated, chips</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where data comes from</td><td><code>dagre</code> across, <code>round-taxi</code></td><td>Boxes</td><td>Colour switch, two legends</td></tr>
        <tr><td>One topic and its ideas</td><td><code>rings</code></td><td>Dots</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What one failure reaches</td><td><code>dagre</code>, a focus</td><td>Dots</td><td>Depth, whole map, stats</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Everything at once</td><td><code>islands</code>, clusters</td><td>Dots</td><td>Minimap, search, chips, full screen</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
